	abstract = {As LLMs increasingly function as collaborative agents within intelligent educational environments, their capacity to deliver equitable, empathetic, and adaptive support becomes central to ethical AI deployment. This study presents an affect-Aware AI educator designed using GenderMag personas and principled engagement metrics to address fairness and inclusivity challenges in AI-Assisted learning. Focusing on two learning scenarios involving identity-related vulnerability and low self-confidence, we evaluated interactions with Abi (female, underrepresented), Tim (male, majority), and Pat (neurodiverse). Comparative analysis demonstrates marked improvements over baseline commercial LLMs, including balanced empathy scores, reduced stereotype signaling, and enhanced motivational scaffolding for marginalized learners. The system exhibits persona-sensitive adaptation while maintaining transparent and explainable response strategies. These results highlight how intentional prompt engineering, affective modeling, and ethical design principles can improve trustworthiness and collaboration in AI tutors. The study aligns with emerging applications of foundational models in education and advances research in XAI, human-centered AI, and inclusive intelligent educational systems. © 2026 IEEE.},
