	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044268738?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Arkansas Tech University, Russellville, AR, United States},
	abstract = {Large language models (LLMs) have great potential for educational tutoring, but hallucinations, poor curriculum alignment, and limited access to domain specific knowledge limit their application in academic settings. Retrieval augmented generation (RAG) is one way to overcome these shortcomings by anchoring model outputs to external sources. Still, most educational RAG systems are limited to single turn question answering and offer little pedagogical assistance. The paper will provide the design and assessment of the RAG enhanced LLM chatbot in Socratic tutoring and adaptive quiz generation at Arkansas Tech University (ATU). The system employs a hybrid retrieval pipeline with FAISS, BM25, and cross encoder re ranking on a knowledge base of 2,328 chunks from 17 open educational resources textbooks, as well as a retriever fine tuned on 929 domain specific query passage pairs. The Qwen3 8B model supports both tutoring and assessment. In tutoring mode, the system generates grounded explanations and Socratic follow up questions; in assessment mode, it generates curriculum aligned multiple choice and short answer questions with immediate feedback. The assessment of 14 questions demonstrated strong results in relevance, conciseness, contextual grounding, and resistance to hallucinations, with overall heuristic and LLM scores of 4.00/5. Lower scores in empathy and Socratic scaffolding indicate areas for future improvement. The findings show the potential of retrieval based tutoring and self adaptive assessment in a university specific curriculum. © 2026 IEEE.},
