	affiliations = {Sousse University, ISITC, PRINCE Research Laboratory, Hammam Sousse, Tunisia},
	abstract = {Artificial Intelligence (AI) has transformed educational assessment through real-time feedback, adaptive learning, and automated evaluation mechanisms. However, existing AI-driven assessment models primarily focus on correctness-based grading, failing to capture cognitive engagement, problem-solving strategies, and AI-assisted modifications. This study introduces IntelliFrame, an ontology-based AI-driven framework designed to improve accuracy, adaptability, and transparency in AI-assisted assessments across multiple disciplines. Unlike traditional models, IntelliFrame systematically evaluates student interactions with AI tools, tracking decision-making processes and iterative learning behaviors to enable process-oriented assessment. A controlled study with 312 undergraduate students demonstrated significant cognitive improvements, including a 32% increase in structured problem-solving (p < 0.01, Cohen’s d = 0.78), an 18% gain in independent critical reasoning (p < 0.05, Cohen’s d = 0.62), and a 27% enhancement in task refinement ability (p < 0.01, Cohen’s d = 0.71). Additionally, 81% of students found IntelliFrame’s adaptive feedback mechanisms beneficial, while 74% improved their ability to assess AI-generated content critically. Faculty members observed a 42% reduced grading workload, highlighting IntelliFrame’s impact on assessment efficiency and real-time progress tracking. Designed for scalability and cross-disciplinary application, IntelliFrame extends beyond programming education to business analytics, legal studies, and medical education. It integrates seamlessly with Intelligent Tutoring Systems (ITS) and AI-driven peer assessment models, further enhancing personalized learning pathways. While IntelliFrame improves adaptive assessment accuracy, challenges remain in bias mitigation, explainability of AI-generated feedback, and optimizing task complexity. Future research should refine bias-detection algorithms, enhance interoperability with learn