	affiliations = {Adobe Research, San Jose, CA, United States; Adobe Research, Seattle, WA, United States},
	abstract = {Elemental dynamic effects like explosions, trails of smoke, splashes of water, and shockwaves are used in animation to communicate motion, impact, and atmosphere. Much like colors on a painters' palette, these effects are frequently reused, maintaining their identity but varying in spatiotemporal placement. Inspired by the metaphor between painting and effects animation, we collected 49 short animations and 42 tutorials to derive design goals for an elemental dynamics palette-and-brush interface. Based on these considerations, we defined the actionbrushes interaction model and implemented it in ActionCanvas: consisting of a semantically editable particle effects palette, anchored and freehand brush heads, and parameter-linking for control over temporal variation. We use ActionCanvas as a probe to explore strengths and limitations of our interaction model, and report early findings from an exploratory study suggesting that further scaffolding effect creation is a promising direction for future work. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
