	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041559549?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Mangalore Insitute of Technology and Enginneering, Department of Artifical Intelligence and Machine Learning, Moodabidri, India; ST Joseph Enginnering College, Department of Intelligent Computing and Business Systems, Vamannjoor, India},
	abstract = {Traditional digital learning systems often do not adequately foster higher-order thinking, adaptability, and personalized feedback. These platforms usually evaluate learning via fixed-response assessments and static grading, which provide limited insight into a learner's reasoning, confidence, or engagement patterns. Furthermore, their dependence on cloud-based infrastructures gives rise to concerns regarding privacy, scalability, and offline access. In this study, we put forward a hybrid intelligent tutoring framework that fuses Socratic questioning through offline-capable large language models (LLMs) with adaptive machine learning methods for assessing performance. The system takes on the role of a reflective tutor by producing tailored, open-ended prompts and simultaneously examining learners' answers for sentiment, depth of reasoning, and behavioural metrics like response time. The system uses models such as logistic regression, recurrent neural networks (RNNs), and gradient-based optimizers to dynamically interpret student performance and adjust learning pathways in real time. Our method combines dialog-driven reasoning with predictive analytics to offer a new, scalable, and privacy-conscious alternative to traditional learning platforms, with the goal of promoting understanding and learner autonomy. © 2026 IEEE.},
