	affiliations = {Hasso Plattner Institute, Potsdam, Germany},
	abstract = {Retrieval practice is a well-established learning strategy that enhances long-Term retention, yet its implementation at scale remains challenging due to the manual effort required for question creation. This paper introduces the AI Recap Interactive Assistant (ARIA), a GPT-based system designed to generate retrieval-based quiz items dynamically, provide immediate feedback, and enable personalized, objective-driven learning. Unlike conventional retrieval tools that rely on static question banks, ARIA continuously generates new, adaptive questions tailored to course objectives, thereby reducing instructor workload and increasing engagement in large-scale educational environments. To evaluate ARIA's usability and pedagogical alignment, we conducted a pilot study with eight experienced programming educators in the context of a Python MOOC. Educators found the system intuitive and valued its objective-based question selection but noted challenges in difficulty calibration and the accuracy of AI-generated feedback, particularly in code-focused tasks. Despite these limitations, participants expressed strong support for ARIA's potential to enhance retrieval practice by providing scalable, on-demand assessments. Our findings indicate that AI-driven question generation can serve as a viable alternative to traditional manual quiz design, though refinements in question validity and adaptive difficulty scaling are necessary for broader adoption. This study contributes to the growing field of AI-enhanced learning at scale by exploring how large language models can automate retrieval practice while maintaining pedagogical integrity.  © 2025 IEEE.},
