	affiliations = {Shenyang Ligong University, Liaoning, Shenyang, 110159, China},
	abstract = {The traditional Intelligent Tutoring Systems (ITS) used in English reading comprehension are based on static and prescriptive hint repositories that are not capable of adapting hints to the individual learner’s misconceptions. In this paper, we propose the LLM-based Adaptive Hinting System (AHS), which is a three-module prompt engineering pipeline: Chain-of-Thought Diagnostic Analyzer for diagnosing errors, a three-level pedagogical scaffolding generator, namely Vocabulary Scaffolding, Contextual Scaffolding, and Structural Scaffolding, and an Answer-Leakage Guardrail to prevent inadvertent disclosure of answers. We consider the AHS when compared to two baselines: a static-hint ITS and a direct-answer LLM, on four metrics: Answer Leakage Rate (ALR), Hint Specificity Score (HSS), Difficulty Alignment Index (DAI), and a composite Pedagogical Quality Score. The AHS achieves an ALR of 2.0% (versus 37.0% for the direct-answer LLM), an HSS of 0.733 (versus 0.551 and 0.217 for the baselines), a DAI of 0.549, and a composite score of 0.752. There is a significant difference between all improvements and baselines (Wilcoxon signed-rank, p < 0.001). With the guardrail, potential answer leakage dropped from 8.0% to 2.0%. Per-question-type analysis reveals that the weakest questions are those that require inferences (HSS = 0.662) and the strongest are those requiring vocabulary (HSS = 0.806), showing those areas where prompting can be enhanced. Implications for Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD) aligned tutoring, the importance of the challenge of error diagnosis in determining the quality of hints, and future challenges in hallucination mitigation and cultural sensitivity are discussed. © The Author(s) under exclusive licence to The Society for Reliability Engineering, Quality and Operations Management (SREQOM), India and The Division of Operation and Maintenance, Lulea University of Technology, Sweden 2026.},
	author_keywords = {Adaptive Scaffolding; Intelligent Tutoring Systems; Large Language Models; Prompt Engineering; RACE Dataset; Reading Comprehension},
