	affiliations = {Saveetha School of Engineering, Saveetha Institute of Medical and Technical Sciences, (SIMATS), Saveetha University, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Chennai, 602105, India},
	abstract = {This study investigates the efficacy of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) in enhancing cognitive programming skills specifically problem decomposition, algorithmic thinking, and debuggingamong low-performing novice learners. We developed and deployed a structured pedagogical intervention, the Generative AI-Driven Cognitive Scaffolding (GAIDCS) framework, which integrates a Large Language Model (LLM) tutor into a 12-week introductory Python course. The framework employs prompt engineering to guide the AI in providing Socratic questioning, incremental code generation, and misconceptiontargeted feedback, rather than direct solutions. Participants (N=1 2 0) were divided into an experimental group (n=60) using the GAIDCS framework and a control group (n=60) using traditional teaching aids. Cognitive skill proficiency was measured via a validated pre-test and post-test instrument. Additionally, learning traces (prompt history, code versions) were analyzed for metacognitive engagement. Results demonstrated a statistically significant improvement in the experimental group. Post-test scores for problem decomposition showed an 82.3 % accuracy rate (vs. 58.7 % control), algorithmic logic scored 7 8. 9% (vs. 5 5. 1% control), and debugging efficiency improved to 7 6. 5% (vs. 5 1. 2% control). A paired samples t-test revealed a large effect size (Cohen's d=1.45, p<0.001). Furthermore, analysis of learning traces indicated a 65 % increase in metacognitive prompts ('explain your approach') issued by learners in the later weeks, correlating with higher post-test gains (r=0.71). These findings suggest that strategically scaffolded GenAI interactions can significantly enhance core cognitive programming skills by promoting active reasoning and mitigating overreliance. The study posits that the key to efficacy lies not in the AI's capability to generate code, but in its structured role as a reflective partner that externalizes and guides the problem-solving process for struggling learners. © 2026 IEEE.},
