	abstract = {Debugging remains a difficult skill for many novice programmers and often requires timely support to prevent frustration and disengagement. To address this challenge, we introduce Scaffl.ed, an intelligent tutoring system that supports Java debugging through adaptive, LLM-driven scaffolding. The system detects student confusion during debugging using a Random Forest model trained on multimodal features from gaze and mouse interactions, after which a robot tutor powered by a Large Language Model (LLM) named Scaffy provides context-aware feedback. Results show that the model consistently outperforms the majority class baseline in both accuracy and F1 score, with gaze-based features, particularly AOI-level features, contributing most to performance. A usability evaluation further suggests that the system demonstrates initial usability and acceptability for educational use. Limitations and directions for future work are discussed. © 2026 IEEE.},
