	abstract = {The highly inflected, rule-based nature of German grammar, such as the case system, presents a significant cognitive load for A1/A2 learners, a challenge existing CALL (Computer Assisted Language Learning) applications often fail to address with deep, Vygotskyan diagnostic feedback. While Large Language Models (LLMs) like Gemini promise a path toward creating such personalized tutors, this paper challenges the prompt-only (Soft Control) approach. We argue that this method is fundamentally unreliable for this type of instruction. We present the design and findings of a Hybrid Tutor MVP (Minimal Viable Prototype). This architecture combines the conversational flexibility of an LLM with a hard-coded programmatic firewall that pre-filters input, and a Vygotskyan scaffolding engine that strictly defines pedagogical logic. Our testing revealed that the prompt-only model is prone to pedagogical hallucinations—critical failures where the LLM misdiagnoses out-of-scope errors (e.g., verb conjugation) as in-scope errors (e.g., plural nouns), providing actively detrimental feedback. We demonstrate that iterative prompt refinement (Soft Control) was insufficient to fix these hallucinations, concluding that a Hybrid Architecture (using Hard Control) is needed to ensure the pedagogical stability and reliability required in a rule-based CALL environment. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.},
	author_keywords = {AI hallucination; hybrid architecture; hybrid control; Rule-based CALL; Vygotskyan Scaffolding},
	keywords = {Architecture; Computer aided instruction; Deep learning; E-learning; Feedback; Iterative methods; Learning systems; Teaching; AI hallucination; Cognitive loads; Computer assisted language learning; Hybrid architectures; Hybrid controls; Language model; Rule based; Rule-based computer assisted language learning; Soft control; Vygotskyan scaffolding; Scaffolds},
