	affiliations = {Portland State University, Electrical and Computer Engineering Dept., Portland, OR, United States},
	abstract = {Individual tutoring is among the most effective ways to enhance student learning by providing immediate, personalized feedback and problem-solving guidance. However, scaling such one-on-one interaction to large student cohorts remains a significant challenge in higher education. This paper reports on the initial development and deployment of a domain-specific AI tutor tailored for a specialized technical course in microwave circuit design which is taken by senior-level undergraduate and graduate students. The tutor, built upon Gemini Gems large language model (LLM) architecture, provides students with 24/7 access to technical support and resource navigation. A key feature of this implementation is the use of the Socratic method; rather than providing solutions, the tutor is instructed to guide students through the underlying design and analysis via targeted questioning. Recent studies in physics demonstrated that such AI-based tutoring can be beneficial to learning. This study details the specific instruction sets used to ground the AI in microwave engineering principles and Socratic dialogue. We present initial findings on student perceptions regarding the tool's helpfulness and accuracy during exam preparation and conclude with suggestions for improvements and the potential for implementation in similar engineering courses.  © 2026 IEEE.},
