	affiliations = {University Of New South Wales Sydney, School Of Computer Science And Engineering, Sydney, Australia},
	abstract = {As computing education scales globally, traditional teaching models struggle to provide personalized support to increasingly diverse student populations with limited instructional resources. This paper presents an AI-driven Intelligent Teaching Assistant platform designed to address course misalignment in AI-based programming tutors, where generated assistance diverges from instructor-intended terminology and pedagogical sequencing. The system features a novel modular agent architecture with two parallel workflows. The instructor-facing workflow employs specialized agents including summarizers, planners, teachers, and quiz generators that automatically transform course materials into structured learning resources. The student-facing workflow integrates a Retrieval-Augmented Generation powered AI tutor into a web-based integrated development environment, providing context-aware coding assistance strictly grounded in course-specific lecture slides with an explicit refusal policy when queries fall outside indexed content. The system has been fully implemented using lecture materials from an introductory C programming course. We present use case scenarios demonstrating how the system handles compiler error interpretation, logic debugging, and concept clarification while maintaining Socratic pedagogical principles. We describe our evaluation methodology comparing the RAG-grounded tutor against a generic ChatGPT baseline to assess course alignment, learning outcomes, and student trust. © 2026 IEEE.},
