	affiliations = {Department of Informatics, University of Bari Aldo Moro, Bari, Italy; Department of Engineering, University of Sannio, Benevento, Italy; Department of Law and Digital Society, UnitelmaSapienza University, Rome, Italy; University of Guelph, Guelph, ON, Canada},
	abstract = {This paper describes UML Miner, a learner-centered platform for UML modeling education that operationalizes Vygotsky’s Zone of Proximal Development and Cognitive Load Theory through an observe–compare–explain loop. Implemented as a Visual Paradigm plugin, the system logs modeling actions, applies declarative process discovery and conformance checking to locate strengths and misconceptions, and turns these findings into transparent, context-sensitive feedback via Retrieval-Augmented Generation with Large Language Models. The contribution is conceptual/system: we articulate the pedagogical rationale and architecture; show how process analytics are distilled into instructional descriptors; and outline guidance policies (minimal sufficient hints, pacing, and fading) that reduce extraneous load and foster autonomy. We also sketch a roadmap toward reliable and inclusive tutoring, including lightweight quality checks and evidence-tagged explanations for generative outputs, adaptive learner modeling for next-step selection, and coordinated (distributed) scaffolding delivered in a single concise voice. The approach supports metacognition and provides instructors with concise diagnostic indicators, and we outline an evaluation plan across distinct learner populations. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.},
