	year = {2026},
	journal = {IEEE Global Engineering Education Conference, EDUCON},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/EDUCON67543.2026.11574485},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044400823?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Prince Mohammad Bin Fahd University, College Of Sciences And Human Studies, Al Khobar, Saudi Arabia; University Of Wolverhampton, School Of Architecture, Computing & Engineering, Wolverhampton, United Kingdom},
	abstract = {One of the most significant developments in Artificial Intelligence (AI) over the past five years has been the adaptation of Large Language Models (LLMs) to a wide range of learning tasks. LLMs are the foundation of powerful AI systems that leverage massive datasets to generate human-like text, images, audio, and video, and are rapidly transforming the landscape of engineering education. Specifically, generative AI (GenAI) employs a wide range of AI models to simulate human-level intelligence. Using machine-learning algorithms, cognitively demanding tasks can be performed with efficiency and proficiency beyond human levels. This paper reports on the initial stages of ongoing research on the application of GenAI in higher education. It examines recent studies to determine the current state of research on learning and teaching with such AI tools, detailing categories of educational AI applications, outlining technological and pedagogical approaches to learning and teaching with AI, and uncovering learner and instructor perceptions of educational AI. Specifically, it addresses these three primary research questions: (1) What kinds of educational GenAI applications are explored in the literature? (2) How are GenAI tools being used in relation to dialogic and conversational pedagogies? (3) What are learner and educator perspectives on using GenAI in education? The research employs a mixed-methods approach, combining a bibliometric analysis with a systematic literature review. Recent reviews that examine a combined total of over 2,000 studies are evaluated to identify those on the use of LLMs in education, thereby providing a synthesis of these reviews. Also, reviews of theories of conversational and dialogic pedagogies, specifically Vygotsky's sociocultural learning, the Socratic method, and Laurillard's conversational framework, are examined. The initial findings suggest that emergent GenAI applications for learning and teaching fall into two main categories: (1) adaptive learning and personalized tutoring; (2) intelligent assessment and management. Additionally, LLMs and chatbots have the potential to serve as ubiquitous tutors that adapt to individual queries, engaging students in ways that motivate them and foster deeper understanding. © 2026 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {active learning; dialogic pedagogy; generative artificial intelligence (GenAI); large language models (LLMs)},
	keywords = {Computer aided instruction; Education computing; Learning algorithms; Learning systems; Machine learning; Teaching; Active Learning; Artificial intelligence systems; Artificial intelligence tools; Dialogic pedagogy; Generative artificial intelligence; High educations; Language model; Large language model; Learning and teachings; Learning tasks; Engineering education},
