	affiliations = {John F. Kennedy Memorial Hs, NJ, Iselin, United States; Thomas Jefferson Hs for S&t, Alexandria, VA, United States; Monroe Township High School, Monroe, NJ, United States; South Brunswick High School, Monmouth Junction, NJ, United States; George Mason University, Information Sciences and Technology Department, United States},
	abstract = {This project aims to develop Argumentation and Graph-based Open Student Modeling Software (ARGOS), an Intelligent Tutoring System (ITS) that constructs a dynamic Open Student Model (OSM) based on real-time conversation. Researchers have explored multiple methods to model student understanding. However, none provide real-time Socratic dialogue while continuously updating a quantitative representation of the student's knowledge. This paper presents two pre-experiments performed to determine the best Socratic tutor for ARGOS and its capabilities. The initial pre-experiment tested the ability of ChatGPT 4o, Claude Sonnet 4, Grok 3, Gemini 2.5 Pro, and Meta AI to guide students and found that Gemini 2.5 Pro scored the highest (26.625/30) on a custom rubric evaluating educational quality, factual precision, and instructional adherence. A pre-experiment was then conducted to assess whether Gemini can accurately assess a student's understanding through rubric-based grading in the areas of accuracy, clear reasoning, clarity, relevance, and effort. Gemini and four researchers graded eight mock conversations in math and history, with the scores showing a strong correlation with human graders (r=0.837). However, Gemini applied stricter grading standards and deviated more from human grading patterns in the humanities. These findings guide the development of ARGOS. The system features a knowledge tracking engine, powered by the Gemini 2.5 Pro API, which evaluates learning progress through a rubric-based assessment using Socratic dialogue. This is supported by an architecture using a React front-end, a FastAPI back-end, and a Firestore database.  © 2025 IEEE.},
