	doi = {10.1016/j.compedu.2026.105706},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105043380026?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Department of Information Communication, Yuan-Ze University, Taiwan; Department of Computer Science and Information Engineering, National Central University, Taiwan; Cross College Elite Tech Program, National Kaohsiung University of Science and Technology, Taiwan; Computer Science & Information Management, Soochow University, Taiwan; Graduate Institute of Network Learning Technology, National Central University, Taiwan},
	abstract = {Computer simulations can support science learning by making scientific phenomena visible and manipulable, but students often struggle to connect simulation observations with underlying mechanisms. This study draws on Cognitive Apprenticeship (CA) theory to design and examine a CA-based generative AI (GenAI) pedagogical agent (CA agent) that provides proactive and contextualized scaffolding during simulation-based learning. Using a contrastive comparison, the study compared the CA agent with a reactive Q&A agent. Data from 53 high school students included pre- and post-tests, student–agent dialogues, students' simulation actions, and the agent's internal reasoning records. Quantitative analyses, dialogue coding, Epistemic Network Analysis (ENA), and qualitative case analyses were used to examine learning processes and outcomes. The ENA and case analyses revealed four patterns of students' epistemic engagement with the CA agent: epistemic outsourcing, the observation–modeling gap, AI-mediated model initiation, and progressive scientific modeling. In addition, the agent's evaluation levels were associated with students' learning outcomes, while case studies of the evaluation records showed that the CA agent's reasoning process was adaptive to the dialogue context but remained primarily concept-oriented, sometimes overlooking students' observational difficulties and their need to exercise epistemic agency. Compared with the reactive Q&A agent, the CA agent showed a marginal advantage in supporting application-oriented learning, and students' application gains varied across different epistemic engagement patterns. This study contributes to research on GenAI-supported simulation-based learning by clarifying how CA-agent scaffolding was associated with students' epistemic engagement and by revealing the affordances and limitations of the CA agent. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd},
