	affiliations = {School of Computing and Engineering, University of Derby, Derbyshire, Derby, United Kingdom},
	abstract = {This paper explores how Generative AI (GenAI) can provide minimally intrusive scaffolding in introductory programming education. The study investigates how students perceive and engage with GenAI tutoring that aims to sustain autonomy while offering timely, adaptive support. A prototype GenAI tutor was integrated into a first-year programming clinic, where 9 students worked on short Python tasks under consistent experimental conditions. The system followed a conservative intervention policy-posing reflective, question-based prompts after brief inactivity. Using qualitative data from system logs, field observations, and post-activity reflections, a reflexive thematic analysis identified three key patterns. First, short-delay interventions were generally perceived as supportive rather than disruptive. Second, open-ended guiding questions were more effective than direct hints in stimulating reflection and self-correction. Third, post-run feedback encouraged sense-making and consolidation of concepts within experiential learning cycles. The findings provide early, small-scale evidence about how learners experience minimally intrusive GenAI support in authentic programming tasks. We explicitly treat these results as exploratory and hypothesis-generating, outlining design and evaluation directions for future work that will incorporate quantitative performance measures and controlled baseline comparisons. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
