	abstract = {This study investigates the effectiveness of an Universal Design for Learning (UDL)-oriented Intelligent Tutoring Systems (ITS) compared to a generic chatbot in educational context, with a particular focus on the moderating role of digital competencies. A case-control experimental design was employed with 99 participants from the TFA support VIII cycle at the University of Foggia (control group: n = 55; experimental group: n = 44). The UDL-based ITS incorporated three core modules - Student, Domain, and Teaching - utilizing Mixture of Agents methodology with advanced language models (GPT-4, Mixtral 8x7b, Meta Llama 3 8B) to provide personalized, adaptive learning experiences. Primary outcomes examined global affectivity (Self-Assessment Manikin), perceived academic self-efficacy, and usability (System Usability Scale, Chatbot Usability Scale). Digital competencies were assessed using the MyDigiSkills questionnaire. Results revealed complex interaction patterns between digital competencies and intervention effectiveness. Information Literacy emerged as a robust predictor across all usability dimensions (standardized coefficients: 0.215–0.450), with a significant negative interaction effect between group membership and Information Literacy. (B = −2.251, p =.003), suggesting compensatory effects particularly benefiting users with lower digital competencies. Problem-solving skills significantly predicted conversation quality (B = 2.019, p =.010). Both systems exceeded good usability thresholds, though no significant differences were found in self-efficacy between groups (U = 1274.500, p =.650). Multivariate analysis revealed significant dimensional effects in affective measures (F(1.624, 157.571) = 9.266, p <.001, η<sup>2</sup>p = 0.087) with substantial temporal stability (rs range: 0.458–0.604). These findings indicate that UDL-oriented ITS provide differential benefits based on users’ digital literacy levels, supporting adaptive scaffolding strategies for inclusive educational technology design. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.},
	author_keywords = {Digital Competencies; Educational Technology; Intelligent Tutoring Systems; Self-Efficacy; Universal Design for Learning; Usability},
