	abstract = {Cultivating learners' higher-order thinking skills through artificial intelligence (AI) poses a significant challenge in open education environments. To address the limitation of existing educational agents - which often prioritize providing direct answers over fostering deep thinking - the paper proposes an innovative design named the Socratic-Metacognitive Framework (SMF). This framework systematically integrates metacognitive theory and the Socratic questioning method, aiming to shape large language models (LLMs) into intelligent tutors capable of stimulating students' metacognitive awareness and critical thinking through strategic questioning. The paper elaborates on the framework's four core design principles and its modular architecture, including Input Parsing and Contextual Awareness, a Socratic Questioning Strategy Library, a Strategy Selector, and a Response Generation and Presentation Module. Through a complete case study in English argumentative essay writing within an open education context, the paper clearly demonstrates how the framework effectively guides students from simplistic viewpoints toward the construction of rigorous, evidence-based arguments via multi-turn dialogues. This work provides a solid theoretical paradigm and an actionable technical pathway for developing next-generation "guidance-oriented"educational AI systems.  © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
