	affiliations = {Khoury College of computer science Northeastern, San Jose, CA, United States},
	abstract = {This tutorial, P2P: From Prompt to Prototype, addresses the persistent "implementation gap" in Human-Computer Interaction (HCI) research, the bottleneck between conceptual generative design and the deployment of functional, testable user interfaces. While Large Language Models (LLMs) have democratized code generation, existing workflows often lack technical grounding, leading to design-code divergence and accessibility oversights. We introduce a hands-on framework that utilizes the Model Context Protocol (MCP) to bridge this gap. By establishing a standardized architectural layer between LLMs and external design systems, APIs, and local development environments, participants learn to transform natural language intent into production-ready React and HTML prototypes. The session follows a scaffolded pedagogical approach: moving from foundational prompt engineering to complex state management and full-system integration. Participants will generate three distinct deliverables, an analytics dashboard, an accessible form, and a data-connected application, while performing automated evaluation via Lighthouse to ensure compliance with WCAG 2.1 standards. This tutorial offers a replicable methodology for researchers and practitioners to maintain human-in-the-loop oversight while leveraging the speed of generative AI for rapid, resilient interface prototyping. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
