	affiliations = {Beijing Normal-Hong Kong Baptist University, Center for Foreign Languages, Zhuhai, China},
	abstract = {This qualitative study examines the impact of Large Language Models (LLMs) on academic writing among English as a Second Language (ESL) students at a top-tier Chinese university. Through longitudinal surveys and thematic analysis of participants' responses collected at four stages of an LLMs-integrated writing course, the research explores students' evolving perceptions. The findings indicate that students perceive LLMs as multifunctional collaborators, fulfilling three key roles: writing assistants that streamline technical aspects of composition, virtual tutors that provide iterative scaffolding, and brainstorming partners that facilitate ideation. These roles collectively enhance writing efficiency, improve output quality, and offer affective support. However., challenges include technical limitations (e.g., generic outputs, factual inaccuracies), over-reliance risks (e.g., diminished critical thinking), and disciplinary mismatches. To effectively incorporate LLMs into writing pedagogy, the study recommends systematically embedding LLMs literacy instruction that encompasses prompt formulation techniques, identification of potential biases, and ethical usage principles. Educators play a crucial mediating role by critically evaluating and adapting LLMs -generated content to align with disciplinary writing standards., while reserving higher-order cognitive tasks for student development. These findings suggest a balanced approach: using LLMs' technical strengths while preserving the core teaching focus that nurtures students' analytical thinking and original expression through consistent human guidance. © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {academic writing; advantages; challenges; Large Language Models (LLMs)},
	keywords = {Computer aided instruction; Curricula; E-learning; Education computing; Iterative methods; Teaching; Technical writing; Academic writings; Advantage; Benefit and challenges; Challenge; English as a second languages (ESL); Language model; Large language model; Qualitative study; Student's voices; University students; Students},
