	abstract = {The United States Air Force (USAF) faces increasing challenges in pilot training due to rising demands on instructor pilots (IPs), the need for scalable training systems, and the diversity of student learning needs. Traditional instructional methods, while effective, are constrained by time, resources, and the ability to provide personalized guidance to individual students. To address these challenges, the USAF is developing IP-GPT, an adaptive tutoring tool leveraging generative Artificial Intelligence (AI) and multi-agent technology to complement existing training pipelines. IP-GPT enhances pilot training by providing dynamic, tailored instruction through a hybridized Large Language Model (LLM) architecture linked to authoritative USAF resources such as flight manuals, technical publications, and standardized courseware. The system ensures accuracy and operational alignment by applying cognitive guardrails and providing contextually relevant responses. Central to IP-GPT’s design is its multi-agent system, which offers diverse learning modalities, including quick answers, Socratic dialogue, and quiz-based assessments, to suit individual learning objectives and preferences. Currently in its early development stages, IP-GPT is undergoing iterative refinement. Lessons learned will be shared, which include, but are not limited to, overcoming gaps in training data, aligning outputs with operational requirements, and addressing policy concerns for AI use in military aviation training. Additionally, future directions and next steps will be discussed as the long-term vision for IP-GPT is to create a scalable, adaptive systems that integrates with a Learning Management System (LMS) to enhance training effectiveness across varied USAF domains and accompany pilots throughout their training journeys. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.},
