	affiliations = {Changsha Normal University, Changsha, China; Guangdong University of Foreign Studies, Guangzhou, China},
	abstract = {In the context of the modern educational transformation driven by artificial intelligence and big data, traditional methods of teaching mathematics often struggle to generate classroom questions that effectively stimulate higher-order thinking and cognitive development. To address this, this study presents a novel framework for problem-driven instructional design based on generative AI technologies. Drawing on Bruner's theory of disciplinary structure and Piaget's theory of cognitive development, we have developed a five-layer recursive dialogic model for AI-generated questioning using the concept of derivatives as a case in point. The framework incorporates prompt engineering and large language models (LLMs) to generate hierarchically structured basic questions that encourage a deeper understanding of concepts. Our approach integrates teacher intention modelling with AI-driven semantic parsing to enable adaptive knowledge scaffolding and real-time cognitive feedback. Empirical examples demonstrate that AI-generated content (AIGC) can assist educators in optimizing the logical progression of instruction while simultaneously triggering metacognitive transfer in learners. This interdisciplinary research bridges the gap between AI and pedagogy, showcasing how intelligent content generation systems can enhance the design of mathematics instruction and offering a scalable methodology for integrating AI into intelligent tutoring systems and digital learning environments. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
