	affiliations = {Tsinghua University, Beijing, China; Nanyang Institute of Technology, Henan, Nanyang, China},
	abstract = {Garment repurposing is a practical form of everyday sustainability, and online how-to videos make it easy to get started. However, novices often get stuck when tutorials skip key steps or do not fit their specific materials, tools, or mistakes. Recent research on large language models (LLMs) points to new ways to scaffold hands-on making. We used ReFashionPal as a technology probe to explore this opportunity. ReFashionPal embeds an LLM assistant into how-to videos, and we invited 14 participants to use it during repurposing tasks. The results highlight the potential of an AI assistant to provide missing procedural details, help users adapt steps to mismatched materials and tools, support troubleshooting when errors occur, and enable exploratory ideation. We further discuss LLM capabilities through the lens of barriers and motivations in garment repurposing and suggest directions for better supporting garment repurposing as a sustainable practice. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
